<<<
it is never an outright or explicit men-

~

+ tion. Allude to has the same limitations. Examples of the legitimate use are:—We looked at cach other wondering which of us he was alluding to; Though he never uses your name, the allusion to you is obvious; He is obscure only because he so often alludes (or only owing to his frequent allusions) to coniemporary events novo forgotten.
===
it is never an outright or explicit men-

+ tion. *Allude to* has the same limitations. Examples of the legitimate use are:—*We looked at each other wondering which of us he was alluding to; Though he never uses your name, the allusion to you is obvious; He is obscure only because he so often alludes (or only owing to his frequent allusions) to contemporary events now forgotten.*
>>>

<<<
The misuse is seen in:—VWWhen the speaker happened to name Mr. Gladstone, the allusion was received with loud cheers./ The lecturer then alluded at some length to the question of strikes.
===
The misuse is seen in:—*When the speaker happened to name Mr. Gladstone, the allusion was received with loud cheers.*/ *The lecturer then alluded at some length to the question of strikes.*
>>>

<<<
It may be added that allude to is often chosen, out of foolish verbosity, when the direct mean would do better; so When you said ‘ some people cannot tell the truth’, were you alluding to (did you mean) me? ; but this is rather an abuse than a misuse.
===
It may be added that *allude to* is often chosen, out of foolish verbosity, when the direct *mean* would do better; so *When you said 'some people cannot tell the truth', were you alluding to* (did you mean) *me?*; but this is rather an abuse than a misuse.
>>>

